WEBVTT

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:19.060
Leave it to Beaver, starring Barbara Billingsley, Hugh Beaumont, Tony Dow, and Jerry Mathers

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:20.260
as the Beaver.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:26.340
Great, I haven't burned a thing yet.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:31.940
to use knives and forks and napkins.

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:33.930
We might just as well eat inside.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:38.590
Yes, Wally, we're going to use knives and forks and napkins.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.940
Yeah, but hamburgers and corn on the cob,

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:43.810
well, that's mostly grabbing stuff.

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:46.850
Well, we're still going to use knives and forks and napkins.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:48.720
Where are Larry and the beaver?

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.120
They seem to be having a wonderful time.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.740
When I was upstairs, they were playing with Larry's football.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:56.460
In the house?

00:00:58.110 --> 00:00:58.890
Oh, it's all right.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:00:59.970
They're just trying to .

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:00.860
to fill it with water.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:22.230
Hey, June, who's out here?

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:27.460
Are we eating out here tonight?

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:29.560
No, but I thought we could over the weekend.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:29.960
Oh.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.400
Good idea, sort of officially open the ant season.

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:33.550
Hi.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:36.120
Beaver has something he wants to ask you.

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:37.030
What is it?

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:39.850
Well, he'd like to have a friend stay overnight.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:41.260
This weekend?

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:41.860
Uh-huh.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.460
Well, I had kind of a hard week.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.560
I was sort of counting on taking it easy.

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:48.850
Well, dear, I think we owe it to him.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:50.870
And the other parents are always having boys over.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:52.720
Yeah, I guess you're right.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:56.580
Well, I suppose I'm to do the barbecuing, huh?

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:57.280
Uh-huh.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.920
Wouldn't think of depriving you of that privilege.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.460
I'd like to make it as much fun for the beaver as possible.

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:07.590
Oh, and, Ward, Winnie asked you about it tonight at supper.

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:08.370
Would you act surprised?

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:09.830
Oh, don't worry about me.

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:10.710
I'll act surprised.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:13.200
Friend for the weekend?

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:18.150
Well, beaver, I understand you have something to ask me.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:20.400
Yes, Dad, I do.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.440
You're not going to like the idea, Dad.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:23.380
It's real goofy.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:26.280
Wally, I think I can make up my own mind.

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:27.570
What is it, Beave?

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:29.890
Could I have a friend over to spend a night tomorrow?

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:39.080
Well, don't think that's such a goofy idea.

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:44.260
Yeah, but what do you hear who his friend is?

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:46.220
He wants to ask Larry Mondello.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.060
Wally, now what's wrong with Larry Mondello?

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:51.620
I don't know.

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:54.960
Except he's always following us big guys around in school.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:56.900
What did he do that for?

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:58.680
Because he knows we don't like it.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:02:59.960
You don't like Larry Mondello?

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.000
I guess we could, Beaver.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.650
Of course, we'd have to ask his parents first,

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:11.890
make sure it's all right with them.

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:13.030
Oh, that's OK.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:15.380
They're bringing Larry over at 11 o'clock in the morning.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:19.060
In that case, I guess it's all right with everyone.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:20.420
Not me, Dad.

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:23.850
Beaver wants Larry to sleep in my room, in my bed.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.690
I don't want him and that goof messing up all my junk.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:28.770
We don't want to mess up your junk.

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:29.970
Larry's going to bring...

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:31.120
Don't jump to mess up.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:35.060
Wally, it's not going to hurt you to spend one night in the guest room.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:39.190
Oh, and then you can have a friend over yourself one of these days.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:40.760
Gee, thanks. Tomorrow?

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.940
Well, not tomorrow, Wally. We're booked up.

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:52.690
Just hope tomorrow Larry doesn't bring his rap.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:57.220
Larry has a rat?

00:03:58.260 --> 00:03:59.980
Oh, it's just a white rat, Ma.

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:03.200
It was white when he got it. You ought to see it now.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:33.880
Hello, Larry.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:35.300
Hello, Mrs. Cleaver.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:37.900
My mother and father dropped me off by your house here.

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:39.440
I'm sorry we couldn't pick you up,

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:40.660
but our car is being greased.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:42.680
Why didn't your parents come in?

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:46.640
Well, they figured as long as you were going to take care of me,

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:49.550
it'd give me a chance to spend the weekend with my mother's sister.

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:52.330
She's my aunt.

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:55.200
Well, come on in.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:07.000
You seem to have come well-prepared.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:09.020
I got my army men in here.

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:12.930
In the other bag, I got my foot bone, my old pants,

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:14.800
and a dirty shirt to play in.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:17.700
Do you have your pajamas?

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:19.570
There wasn't room for them.

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:22.750
Beaver said I could wear a pair of his.

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:27.520
Beaver?

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:29.960
My foot and my onion suck.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:31.040
the floor in my room.

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:32.510
I got my army man.

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:33.670
Come on, let's go kill him.

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:37.900
Don't you boys go running off any place now without telling me.

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:38.880
We're going to have lunch soon.

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:40.350
Your father's going to barbecue.

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:44.500
Mrs. Cleaver, my mom sent you a box of candy.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:47.290
Well, thank you.

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:49.960
But they're going to have to get you another one.

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:51.780
I sat on it in the car.

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:55.860
Well, Larry, sometimes those things happen.

00:05:57.590 --> 00:05:59.250
We're going to have fun, aren't we, Beaver?

00:05:59.470 --> 00:05:59.970
We sure are.

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:05.700
You know, Dad, it's funny.

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:06.490
What's funny?

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:10.480
Well, whenever we cook inside, mom always does the cooking.

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:13.600
But whenever we cook outside, you always do it.

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:14.060
How come?

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:17.130
Oh, it's sort of traditional, I guess.

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:19.630
You know, they say a woman's place is in the home.

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:22.140
And I suppose as long as she's in the home,

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:23.320
she might as well be in the kitchen.

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:27.600
Oh, well, that explains about mom.

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:29.850
But how come you always do the outside cooking?

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:33.880
Well, I'll tell you, son, women do all right

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:36.080
when they have all the modern conveniences.

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:40.100
But us men are better at this rugged type of outdoor cooking,

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:42.820
sort of a throwback to caveman days.

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:45.300
Hand me those asbestos gloves, would you, Ollie?

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:50.580
Well, there's no sense in us cavemen burning our hands.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:54.980
How are we doing?

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:56.030
Oh, great.

00:06:56.170 --> 00:06:57.110
I haven't burned a thing yet.

00:06:57.940 --> 00:06:58.520
Gee, Mom.

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:02.740
Do we have to use knives and forks and napkins?

00:07:03.140 --> 00:07:04.740
We might just as well eat inside.

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:09.420
Yes, Wally, we're going to use knives and forks and napkins.

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:12.740
Yeah, but hamburgers and corn on the cob,

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:14.630
well, that's mostly grabbing stuff.

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:17.690
Well, we're still going to use knives and forks and napkins.

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:19.550
Where are Larry and the beaver?

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:22.970
Oh, they seem to be having a wonderful time.

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:25.590
When I was upstairs, they were playing with Larry's football.

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:27.330
In the house?

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:29.750
Oh, it's all right.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.720
just tried to fill it with water.

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:35.370
Well, they better not touch any of my stuff.

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:39.070
Well, now, Wally, don't spoil it for your brother.

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:39.950
He has a guest.

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:40.760
OK.

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:43.730
Remember, you said I could have someone over sometime.

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:45.110
Sure, you can, too.

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:46.090
Sometime.

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:48.120
Hey, you better call them down, June.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:49.140
We'll be ready to eat any minute.

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:52.910
Oh, here you are.

00:07:53.070 --> 00:07:53.570
You hungry?

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:56.680
Where's Larry?

00:07:59.080 --> 00:07:59.700
Front porch.

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:03.670
What's he doing that for?

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:05.140
I don't know.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:06.750
You don't mind, Dad.

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:11.320
Beaver, how long has Larry been standing on the front porch?

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:13.150
I don't know.

00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:15.810
About a half hour.

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:21.200
I knew something like this would happen.

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:24.890
Think I'd better see about this.

00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:42.220
Larry, what are you doing out here?

00:08:43.310 --> 00:08:43.670
Nothing.

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:45.460
Something wrong?

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:48.010
Yeah, I want to go home.

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:51.190
You came to spend the night, Larry.

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:52.850
But I don't want to spend the night.

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:54.210
I want to go home.

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:57.320
Well, why do you want to go home?

00:08:58.200 --> 00:08:59.740
Because beaver hit me in the stomach.

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:02.720
I understand my option.

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:06.680
Well, you come on in the house with me.

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:08.500
I'm sure we can straighten this out with Beaver.

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:10.250
I don't want to straighten out nothing.

00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:11.710
I want to go home.

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:15.300
You can't go home right now, Larry.

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:17.280
Your father and mother have gone to visit your aunt.

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:20.340
I could go home to my grandmother's.

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:22.350
She lives over by the high school.

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:24.720
Can't drive you way over there right now.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:26.660
My car is down being greased.

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:29.400
All right, I'll walk.

00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:32.910
I never walked six miles before, but I guess I could.

00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:35.620
I wish I never came over here.

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:38.290
All right, you come on inside.

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:41.120
I wonder what in the world your father

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:42.440
could be doing with Larry.

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:45.950
Hubbit Beeb had a fight with him.

00:09:46.730 --> 00:09:47.550
I did not.

00:09:47.800 --> 00:09:50.260
He had a fight with me, and I punched him in the stomach.

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:51.500
A fight?

00:09:52.070 --> 00:09:53.790
June, will you bring the beaver in here?

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:55.320
Uh-oh.

00:10:08.890 --> 00:10:10.690
It seems the boys had a little disagreement.

00:10:10.930 --> 00:10:11.490
So I heard.

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:13.400
He hit me, Mrs. Cleaver.

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:16.080
He hit me right when I almost had my operation.

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:18.520
I hit you because you wrecked my fort.

00:10:18.970 --> 00:10:20.830
I want to go to my grandmother's.

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:23.180
Wish you never came over here.

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:23.540
All right.

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:24.480
That's enough, boys.

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:27.080
If Larry wants to go to his grandmother's, we'll call a taxi.

00:10:28.330 --> 00:10:29.950
Larry Beaver's your best friend.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:31.370
He is not.

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:32.640
I never liked him at all.

00:10:33.370 --> 00:10:36.350
I only came over here because my parents wanted to visit my aunt.

00:10:36.910 --> 00:10:37.350
Ha!

00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:39.710
You asked me all to come over here.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:41.640
You're the meanest guy in the whole crowd.

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:42.960
Boys, that's enough.

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:44.990
Hey, Dad.

00:10:45.230 --> 00:10:45.450
What?

00:10:45.550 --> 00:10:46.610
I'm on the phone, Wally.

00:10:47.530 --> 00:10:48.350
But, Dad.

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:49.470
What?

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:52.270
I think the hamburgers are burnt.

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:55.230
Well, uh, uh, uh, uh, there it is.

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:12.920
Wally what well who am I talking would you mind sending a taxi over to 485

00:11:12.920 --> 00:11:28.020
Mapleton 485 Mapleton Drive put the iced tea back in the refrigerator

00:11:30.620 --> 00:11:31.200
Not yet.

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:32.400
I called him again.

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:34.040
Guess they're a little tied up.

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:35.740
Where's Larry and the beaver?

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:37.780
Larry went upstairs to make sure

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:38.960
he didn't leave anything of his.

00:11:39.260 --> 00:11:40.660
And then the beaver went up to make sure

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:42.420
that Larry didn't take anything of his.

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:47.000
As long as beaver had to hit him in the stomach,

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:48.840
I don't see why he couldn't have waited until after we

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:49.720
had our hamburgers.

00:11:50.030 --> 00:11:51.070
Well, we tried.

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:55.480
Or there must be an easier way for our boys to make friends.

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:57.610
I guess that's the taxi.

00:11:58.390 --> 00:11:58.930
Hey, Dad.

00:11:59.300 --> 00:11:59.560
Yeah?

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.160
I cranked the grill up so the hamburgers wouldn't burn again.

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:02.880
Oh, fine.

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:04.460
Some of them are starting to curl up.

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:06.260
So am I.

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:09.900
You call a cab?

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:10.560
Twice.

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:12.740
Larry, Larry.

00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:13.900
He'll be right down.

00:12:14.970 --> 00:12:15.370
Larry.

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:20.100
Mr. Cleaver?

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:21.020
The cab's here.

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:22.180
Cab.

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:25.810
The taxi cab to take you to your grandmother's.

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:28.300
Dad, does Larry have to go home?

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:29.980
Well, you starting to have fun.

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:33.390
Have fun?

00:12:33.530 --> 00:12:37.810
I thought you two weren't even speaking to each other.

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:39.520
Oh, that was before.

00:12:42.230 --> 00:12:43.410
Now, just a minute, Larry.

00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:46.400
15 minutes ago, you insisted you

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:48.000
wanted to go to your grandmother's.

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:50.160
Now, do you want to go, or don't you?

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:52.740
Gee, I'm going to stay here, Mr. Cleaver.

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:54.240
Not, Dad.

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:56.450
I promised I could have a friend overnight.

00:12:57.690 --> 00:12:58.230
All right.

00:12:58.710 --> 00:12:59.530
All right, you can.

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:02.230
But only if you're sure you're going

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:03.370
to get along with each other.

00:13:03.550 --> 00:13:05.010
I'm sure they will now, Ward.

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:07.610
Mrs. Cleaver, is the food ready?

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:09.920
All this fussing has made me hungry.

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:13.940
Yes, well, you go on outside.

00:13:13.940 --> 00:13:15.200
I'll get the iced tea.

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:16.930
Come on, ma, let's go.

00:13:20.680 --> 00:13:24.180
Oh, I guess we won't be needing that cab.

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:27.280
Here, here.

00:13:28.690 --> 00:13:29.830
Thanks, thanks for your trouble.

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:31.190
You're welcome.

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:38.940
Well, the hamburgers were cold and the iced tea was warm.

00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:41.460
But as they say, the spirit was jolly.

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:44.020
Well, there's one thing I'll say for Larry.

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:47.320
Punching the stomach didn't affect his appetite any.

00:13:47.780 --> 00:13:49.680
Oh, the way that boy ate.

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:51.940
It was like watching a mongoose.

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:54.390
I don't think I've ever seen anyone

00:13:54.390 --> 00:13:56.010
eat ketchup on corn before.

00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:56.570
Oh.

00:13:57.290 --> 00:13:59.460
Where are they now?

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:02.240
Well, Larry and Beaver are up in their room

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:04.620
playing some sort of a card game called fish

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:06.840
while he's out in the garage taking

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:08.520
the nails out of an old box.

00:14:09.220 --> 00:14:10.260
What's he doing that for?

00:14:11.390 --> 00:14:11.910
I don't know.

00:14:11.990 --> 00:14:13.790
Maybe he needs them to make another box.

00:14:14.380 --> 00:14:14.860
Oh.

00:14:17.230 --> 00:14:22.280
Ward, could Larry and Beaver be hitting each other one minute

00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:24.520
and be playing together the next?

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:26.590
Well, they're just kids.

00:14:26.750 --> 00:14:29.900
They haven't learned to hate on a permanent basis.

00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:37.080
Dad, look at these neat nails I pulled out of that old box

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:37.700
in the garage.

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:39.440
Say, those are neat, aren't they?

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:40.340
Ah.

00:14:41.400 --> 00:14:42.100
Hey, look, Mom.

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:44.690
Wally, what do you need nails for?

00:14:45.090 --> 00:14:46.450
I'm going to give them to Tui Brown.

00:14:46.710 --> 00:14:47.990
He's building a doghouse.

00:14:48.370 --> 00:14:49.710
I didn't know the Browns had a dog.

00:14:50.390 --> 00:14:51.110
They don't.

00:14:52.400 --> 00:14:55.760
Well, the way Tui figures, if he builds a doghouse,

00:14:56.240 --> 00:14:58.680
then maybe his father will let him get a dog to put in it.

00:15:00.220 --> 00:15:05.400
Well, hey, Mom, how come I got to sleep

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:06.600
in the back bedroom tonight?

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:09.070
Because Larry's going to sleep in with the beaver.

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:12.120
Well, why can't they sleep in the back bedroom?

00:15:12.590 --> 00:15:14.010
Because Larry is our guest.

00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:16.710
That's the way it always is.

00:15:17.570 --> 00:15:19.710
Why should a guest get treated better than the guy who lives

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:19.950
here?

00:15:20.070 --> 00:15:21.890
We want our guest to feel at home.

00:15:22.550 --> 00:15:24.970
Boy, beaver really made him feel at home,

00:15:25.010 --> 00:15:25.850
hitting him in the stomach.

00:15:27.010 --> 00:15:29.280
You still got to sleep in the back bedroom tonight,

00:15:29.380 --> 00:15:29.980
and I want everybody to sleep in the back bedroom.

00:15:30.880 --> 00:15:34.350
Okay. Boy, this is really gonna be something.

00:15:34.890 --> 00:15:36.450
Larry in Beaver's pajamas.

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:51.600
Dad? Are you awake, Dad?

00:15:55.520 --> 00:15:57.730
Beaver. It's almost 12 o'clock.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:00.420
What is it?

00:16:00.690 --> 00:16:01.730
Larry says he's sick.

00:16:03.200 --> 00:16:03.540
Sick?

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:05.080
Where's he sick?

00:16:05.590 --> 00:16:07.410
Right down where his stomach starts to grow.

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:09.840
Well, let's see about it.

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:17.780
What's the matter, Larry?

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:20.690
I feel sick right here.

00:16:21.140 --> 00:16:22.880
I think it's one of my appendixes.

00:16:23.890 --> 00:16:26.960
He woke up a while ago and started making noises.

00:16:27.380 --> 00:16:28.460
I thought I'd call you.

00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:32.140
Larry, has this ever happened before?

00:16:32.850 --> 00:16:34.170
Only when I go visiting.

00:16:36.730 --> 00:16:37.510
What's the matter?

00:16:38.690 --> 00:16:39.310
Larry's sick.

00:16:40.100 --> 00:16:40.980
What are those appendixes?

00:16:41.930 --> 00:16:44.740
I don't think you have a fever.

00:16:45.190 --> 00:16:45.990
That's of your tongue.

00:16:50.060 --> 00:16:51.900
Maybe we ought to call the doctor, you know,

00:16:51.980 --> 00:16:53.140
with his parents away and all.

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:55.910
Come on, Larry.

00:16:56.560 --> 00:16:58.060
Here, now, you get some rest.

00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:17.670
Mrs. Cleaver, it was dark in here.

00:17:18.290 --> 00:17:20.920
Gee, Larry, when we ate those two after supper,

00:17:21.080 --> 00:17:22.440
you told me they were all gone.

00:17:23.050 --> 00:17:25.050
I wasn't counting the ones under the pillow.

00:17:26.230 --> 00:17:28.460
My stomach hurts, Mrs. Cleaver.

00:17:29.100 --> 00:17:29.540
Oh.

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:32.880
Larry, has this ever happened to you before?

00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:34.720
Only when he goes visiting.

00:17:35.330 --> 00:17:38.220
I tell you what, Larry, I think you've just overeaten.

00:17:38.980 --> 00:17:40.900
Now then, we're going to turn the light off,

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:42.940
and you lie back and try and go to sleep.

00:17:44.280 --> 00:17:45.680
I'm sure you'll be all right.

00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:47.440
But if you're not, you let us know.

00:17:48.310 --> 00:17:50.460
You do just what we tell you, and then

00:17:50.460 --> 00:17:51.600
I'm sure you'll be fine.

00:17:52.410 --> 00:17:53.050
All right.

00:18:10.840 --> 00:18:15.290
What is it now, Beaver?

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:17.290
I think Larry's all right now.

00:18:18.840 --> 00:18:20.160
Oh, fine.

00:18:20.380 --> 00:18:21.290
But thanks for telling me.

00:18:22.890 --> 00:18:24.460
Did he, uh, do what we told him to?

00:18:24.910 --> 00:18:28.960
Well, he did something, but it wasn't what you told him.

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:34.860
Well, is there something else?

00:18:35.580 --> 00:18:37.780
Yeah, Larry's stomach's kind of empty now.

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:40.740
Who wants to know if he can have his candy bars back?

00:18:42.880 --> 00:18:44.200
No, he cannot.

00:18:44.660 --> 00:18:46.660
Now, come on, both of you get all back to sleep.

00:18:48.750 --> 00:18:50.720
What happened to Larry?

00:18:51.020 --> 00:18:52.480
Nothing new or unique.

00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:03.700
Are you tired?

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:08.340
No more than anyone else would be.

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:09.520
It's been up half the night.

00:19:11.370 --> 00:19:11.770
Ward?

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:12.530
Well?

00:19:13.240 --> 00:19:15.640
Why is it always so much harder to take care of someone

00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:17.320
else's children than it is your own?

00:19:19.270 --> 00:19:22.560
Well, it's always been safer and easier

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:24.740
to ruin your own children than someone else's.

00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:29.980
Hey, Dad, would you go upstairs?

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:31.420
Stares me out, Larry and the Beaver.

00:19:31.690 --> 00:19:33.770
They got their dirty feet all over my bed.

00:19:35.250 --> 00:19:37.330
Wally, Mrs. Mandello, just phone.

00:19:37.430 --> 00:19:39.090
She's going to pick up Larry any minute.

00:19:39.170 --> 00:19:40.770
Now, look, let's not have any more trouble

00:19:40.770 --> 00:19:42.090
until he's out of the house, huh?

00:19:42.730 --> 00:19:43.090
OK.

00:19:44.300 --> 00:19:45.960
But gee, they're just putting their feet up there

00:19:45.960 --> 00:19:47.120
to make me blow my top.

00:19:47.970 --> 00:19:49.950
Wally, now your father said that you could have a guest

00:19:49.950 --> 00:19:50.890
over one of these days.

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:53.230
And when you do, you wouldn't want Beaver to start trouble.

00:19:53.890 --> 00:19:54.610
All right, Mom.

00:19:55.730 --> 00:19:57.750
Hey, is it true Larry got sick last night?

00:19:59.120 --> 00:19:59.900
Rumors to that effect.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:02.040
Gee, I'm sorry I was sleeping.

00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:03.080
I missed all the fun.

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:08.580
Oh, maybe that's Mrs. Mandela.

00:20:09.280 --> 00:20:11.140
I'm going over to shoot baskets at Eddie's.

00:20:11.240 --> 00:20:11.800
So long, Mom.

00:20:12.590 --> 00:20:14.450
Oh, Mrs. Mandela, come right in.

00:20:14.550 --> 00:20:15.110
Thank you.

00:20:15.350 --> 00:20:16.900
Hello, Mrs. Mandela.

00:20:17.060 --> 00:20:20.080
Oh, Mrs. Cleaver, I want to thank you for having Larry over.

00:20:20.240 --> 00:20:23.980
Well, we certainly enjoyed having him, didn't we, Ward?

00:20:24.750 --> 00:20:25.390
Oh, yes.

00:20:25.550 --> 00:20:27.370
Oh, yes, he's a wonderful little fellow.

00:20:27.870 --> 00:20:29.610
He didn't make any trouble, did he?

00:20:30.280 --> 00:20:33.040
Trouble? We hardly knew he was in the house.

00:20:34.760 --> 00:20:38.690
I drive up to our window.

00:20:39.050 --> 00:20:39.950
Hello, dear.

00:20:40.840 --> 00:20:45.560
It's too early.

00:20:45.680 --> 00:20:47.920
We're passing by on our way home.

00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:50.220
Can you stay a while longer?

00:20:53.210 --> 00:20:56.910
We wouldn't want to wear out our welcome, would we, dear?

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:01.680
I guess not.

00:21:02.270 --> 00:21:04.690
Well, come on. Your father's waiting.

00:21:04.890 --> 00:21:05.610
So long, Beafe.

00:21:07.240 --> 00:21:09.980
Oh, thank you.

00:21:11.390 --> 00:21:14.870
Larry, aren't you going to say goodbye to Mr. and Mrs. Cleaver, too,

00:21:14.930 --> 00:21:17.370
and tell him what a nice time you've had visiting them?

00:21:17.790 --> 00:21:19.090
Oh, yeah, goodbye.

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:21.880
And I had a real nice time visiting with you.

00:21:22.280 --> 00:21:24.860
Well, thank you, Larry. We enjoyed having you.

00:21:25.960 --> 00:21:29.040
I'm sorry I got sick from eating, and Beaver hit me on the stomach.

00:21:31.690 --> 00:21:32.770
Larry was ill.

00:21:33.680 --> 00:21:37.780
Oh, well, I'm afraid the boys ate a few too many candy bars.

00:21:38.250 --> 00:21:40.450
And I only punched him because he wrecked my fork.

00:21:41.100 --> 00:21:46.740
Oh, well, thanks again for having Larry.

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:48.680
Yeah, thanks for having me again.

00:21:49.050 --> 00:21:49.910
I'll see you, Beaver.

00:21:50.340 --> 00:21:51.220
Bye, Larry.

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:54.320
Where in the stomach?

00:22:00.800 --> 00:22:03.900
It was a little hectic.

00:22:05.970 --> 00:22:16.740
Larry had fun.

00:22:17.910 --> 00:22:20.090
Ollie had fun, heckling Larry and the beaver.

00:22:21.650 --> 00:22:22.150
Warren?

00:22:22.430 --> 00:22:22.570
Hmm?

00:22:23.140 --> 00:22:24.660
Think we handled everything all right?

00:22:24.800 --> 00:22:26.980
Oh, we must have done our duty as parents.

00:22:27.920 --> 00:22:28.680
I'm exhausted.

00:22:33.040 --> 00:22:34.100
So am I.

00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:44.260
Beaver, put your elbows down.

00:22:44.550 --> 00:22:45.730
You look like a seagull.

00:22:46.250 --> 00:22:46.850
Yes, sir.

00:22:49.060 --> 00:22:50.460
You can eat outside all the time.

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:52.440
So it wouldn't have to be so polite.

00:22:52.720 --> 00:22:54.080
Well, after yesterday, I think we've

00:22:54.080 --> 00:22:55.860
had enough of eating outside for a while.

00:22:56.380 --> 00:22:59.900
Beaver, the idea is to be polite no matter where we eat.

00:23:00.570 --> 00:23:01.150
Yes, Mom.

00:23:04.320 --> 00:23:07.600
Hey, Dad, you said I could have someone over sometime soon.

00:23:08.290 --> 00:23:08.930
That's right, Wally.

00:23:09.050 --> 00:23:10.620
You can have someone over sometime.

00:23:11.170 --> 00:23:11.790
Gee, thanks.

00:23:12.090 --> 00:23:14.070
I'd like to have Eddie Haskell over next weekend.

00:23:16.410 --> 00:23:20.110
Gee, Dad, I don't like Eddie.

00:23:20.440 --> 00:23:21.460
He's always teasing me.

00:23:21.780 --> 00:23:22.920
Never mind, Beaver.

00:23:23.940 --> 00:23:26.080
Wally, next weekend?

00:23:26.360 --> 00:23:29.210
But Beaver just had Larry over, and I'm sure

00:23:29.210 --> 00:23:29.970
your father didn't mean to me.

00:23:30.920 --> 00:23:35.080
Well, gosh, Dad, I practically already asked him.

00:23:35.330 --> 00:23:36.610
And it's all right with his father.

00:23:38.610 --> 00:23:39.990
Well, I'll tell you what, Wally,

00:23:40.760 --> 00:23:43.460
Eddie spends a lot of time over here as it is.

00:23:44.140 --> 00:23:46.820
Wouldn't it be nice if you spent next weekend over at his house?

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:49.940
Good idea, Wally.

00:23:51.430 --> 00:23:54.440
Gosh, Dad, Eddie already asked his father that.

00:23:55.040 --> 00:23:57.660
He said he didn't want us hanging around messing up his weekend.

00:23:57.960 --> 00:23:58.600
I see.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.660
But it's all right for you to hang around here and mess up my weekend.

00:24:02.972 --> 00:24:05.312
Well, gosh, Dad, you're not like Eddie's father.

00:24:05.312 --> 00:24:06.312
Well, you're...

00:24:07.580 --> 00:24:07.920
Wow.

00:24:08.252 --> 00:24:10.412
You're too nice a father to say a thing like that.

00:24:11.164 --> 00:24:12.244
Well, I still think that...

00:24:14.204 --> 00:24:15.044
Eddie said that?

00:24:15.548 --> 00:24:16.048
No, Dad.

00:24:16.288 --> 00:24:17.128
I told him that.

00:24:18.652 --> 00:24:18.972
Oh.

00:24:22.556 --> 00:24:24.556
Well, that's very nice, Wally.

00:24:25.852 --> 00:24:28.572
But I still think your mother should decide about this.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:30.660
How about it, Mom?

00:24:32.390 --> 00:24:36.030
Well, Wally, I think your father is just nice enough

00:24:36.030 --> 00:24:37.910
to put up with Eddie next weekend.

00:24:38.360 --> 00:24:39.300
Gee, thanks, Dad.

00:24:39.580 --> 00:24:41.620
Now I won't have to call him back and tell him it's off.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:02.000
You
